<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lebus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebus</a> is a small town on the Oder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> (Oder). Once a Slavic stronghold, it became in the 12th century the seat of a Polish bishopric under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, which later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/furstenwalde">Fürstenwalde</a>, and it gave its name to the surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-lebus">Land of Lebus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lebus.jpg" alt="Lebus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Totzauer · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lebus_-_Unterstadt_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
